WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.215
MCP capabilities commonly appear as tools, resources, and prompts.

00:00:03.215 --> 00:00:06.676
A tool is a callable operation exposed by a server.

00:00:06.676 --> 00:00:11.522
It may look up information, query a system, create a ticket, summarize a file,

00:00:11.522 --> 00:00:14.291
trigger a workflow, or perform another defined action.

00:00:14.291 --> 00:00:19.137
A resource is data or content made available as context, such as a file,

00:00:19.137 --> 00:00:21.906
schema, record, document, repository item, or application-specific object.

00:00:21.906 --> 00:00:26.751
A prompt is a reusable prompt template or interaction pattern exposed by a server.

00:00:26.751 --> 00:00:30.905
Tools and resources are easy to confuse, but they serve different roles.

00:00:30.905 --> 00:00:34.366
A resource gives the AI application content to reason over.

00:00:34.366 --> 00:00:36.789
A tool does something or requests something.

00:00:36.789 --> 00:00:42.327
Reading an API schema as context is different from calling an operation that changes a system.

00:00:42.327 --> 00:00:45.442
Fetching a document is different from updating a ticket.

00:00:45.442 --> 00:00:46.480
Good courseware, documentation,

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.018
and system design should make those distinctions visible because the risks and approval needs are different.

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:55.480
Prompts are also distinct from user messages and system instructions.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.287
A prompt template may help standardize how a task is framed,

00:00:59.287 --> 00:01:04.133
but it should not be treated as a replacement for policy or access control.

00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:07.940
Tool descriptions, resource metadata, and prompt templates all influence model behavior.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.401
They affect what the model thinks a capability is for,

00:01:11.401 --> 00:01:15.555
when it should be used, and how the result should be interpreted.

00:01:15.555 --> 00:01:17.631
That makes capability metadata operationally important.

00:01:17.631 --> 00:01:20.400
Capabilities should be clear, narrow, documented, and understandable.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.554
A narrowly described read-only lookup is easier to review than a broad

00:01:24.554 --> 00:01:26.977
tool that can perform many unrelated actions.

00:01:26.977 --> 00:01:30.438
Documentation should explain purpose, owner, inputs, outputs, permissions, data sensitivity,

00:01:30.438 --> 00:01:31.476
and expected use.

00:01:31.476 --> 00:01:33.207
This is not just tidiness.

00:01:33.207 --> 00:01:36.322
Clear capability boundaries reduce confusion, make approval decisions easier,

00:01:36.322 --> 00:01:41.860
and help teams scale MCP adoption without losing track of what each integration can actually do.
